        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/9ca5e0c34ea6514a73a1c16147b2c3e71bbf686fb874f92eb18817d3a8c6e0bc.png)\n\nConsider a function $f(t)$ for which the graph of the Laplace transform $L{f(t)}=F(s)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $L{{e}^{6t}f(t)}?$",
        "worked_solution": "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{6t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the right.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/3171a91443332410985bf7e42d54719185c884b9183e55e4ba7e574d6c0b2143.png)",
